    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "7e5ce1cc",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7e5ce1cc"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Intro to Pandas\n",
        "\n",
        "## *Workshop 2*  [![Open In Colab](https://github.com/oballinger/BASC0005/blob/main/colab-badge.png?raw=1)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/oballinger/BASC0005/blob/main/notebooks/W02.%20Pandas.ipynb)\n",
        "\n",
        "In this workshop we get used to working with data imported from external files: we load it with *pandas*, select the bits we're interested in, clean it, and graph it.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Aims\n",
        "\n",
        "- Import data into Python with pandas\n",
        "- Access specific rows, columns and cells\n",
        "- Plot the data, with labelled axes\n",
        "- Find and fix data that has been read in as text\n",
        "- Filter by value\n",
        "- Reshape data from **wide** to **long** format"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "6d5f54f4",
      "metadata": {
        "execution": {
          "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.108477Z",
          "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.108271Z",
          "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.558556Z",
          "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.557990Z"
        },
        "id": "6d5f54f4"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.rcParams[\"figure.figsize\"] = (10, 5)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "fc97396d",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fc97396d"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "We are going to work with some UK income data: income by **percentile**, from the Family Resources Survey 2010-11, collated by the Institute for Fiscal Studies ([where do you fit in?](https://ifs.org.uk/tools_and_resources/where_do_you_fit_in)). The IFS **equivalises** incomes: it adjusts them for household size, because a couple needs more than a single person but not twice as much ([equivalisation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivalisation)).\n",
        "\n",
        "The IFS is quoted in UK Government documents, so we can have some trust in its accuracy. If we were publishing research on this, that wouldn't be good enough: we'd want to understand, and ideally reproduce, its methodology rather than just trust it.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Loading the data\n",
        "\n",
        "`read_csv` can read straight from a URL. We tell pandas to use the first column (the percentile) as the index, which makes life easier."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "8e89dca0",
      "metadata": {
